## Section 7 — Domain Feature Extraction

```spl
| eval entropy_label_digits=replace(entropy_label, "[^0-9]", "")
| eval digit_count=len(entropy_label_digits)
| eval digit_ratio=if(entropy_label_len>0, round(digit_count/entropy_label_len, 3), 0)

| eval entropy_label_vowels=replace(entropy_label, "[^aeiou]", "")
| eval vowel_count=len(entropy_label_vowels)
| eval vowel_ratio=if(entropy_label_len>0, round(vowel_count/entropy_label_len, 3), 0)

| eval has_consonant_run=if(match(entropy_label, "(?i)[bcdfghjklmnpqrstvwxz]{5,}"), 1, 0)
| eval hyphen_count=len(entropy_label)-len(replace(entropy_label, "-", ""))
```

Entropy alone is a strong signal but not definitive. These four additional features supplement it to build a composite picture and reduce false positives from legitimate high-entropy domains.

**digit_ratio**: Strips all non-digit characters and measures what fraction of the label is digits. Legitimate domain labels rarely exceed 10-15% digits. DGA algorithms frequently embed timestamps, counters, or random numbers, pushing this ratio higher. Threshold: `>=0.25` (25% or more of the label is digits).

**vowel_ratio**: Strips all non-vowel characters and measures what fraction of the label is vowels. Natural English text averages around 38-42% vowels. Algorithmically generated strings have no linguistic structure and typically fall well below 20% vowels. The threshold `<0.20` with a minimum label length of `>=8` prevents very short labels from triggering on noise. `y` is intentionally excluded from the vowel set in the consonant run check to reduce false positives on words where `y` functions as a vowel.

**has_consonant_run**: Tests for 5 or more consecutive consonants. This is nearly impossible in natural language — the longest consonant run in common English words is 3-4 (`strengths` has 5 but is an outlier). DGA output routinely produces sequences like `kqwzpj` or `xbrtvnm`. Returns 1 (true) or 0 (false).

**hyphen_count**: Counts hyphens by comparing label length before and after stripping them. Legitimate domain labels occasionally use one hyphen. Two or more hyphens in a label is unusual and commonly seen in algorithmically constructed domains used for CDN abuse, fast-flux infrastructure, or C2 domain generation schemes that separate components with hyphens.

